WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.747
Spring AI provides abstractions for model access, prompts, tools, retrieval, and observability

00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:08.141
that fit a Spring application.

00:00:08.291 --> 00:00:12.740
ChatClient offers a fluent interface for building a request, while annotated Java

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.965
methods can be exposed as tools.

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:18.488
Dependency injection supplies configured model clients.

00:00:18.638 --> 00:00:24.581
Framework convenience does not remove your responsibility for identity, side effects, budgets,

00:00:24.581 --> 00:00:25.571
and evidence.

00:00:25.721 --> 00:00:31.583
The supplied optional adapter pins Spring AI 2.0.2 with Spring Boot 4.0.9

00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:33.048
and Java 21.

00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:38.038
The core agent project intentionally remains small enough to inspect without learning

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:39.248
every framework abstraction.

00:00:39.398 --> 00:00:44.125
Read the version-matched documentation when upgrading: Spring AI 2 introduced changes in

00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:46.882
how the tool loop composes with advisors.

00:00:47.032 --> 00:00:51.248
The optional Spring AI application uses a local Ollama model and one

00:00:51.248 --> 00:00:52.302
read-only status tool.

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:56.646
It runs only when a user explicitly enables the live-demo profile and

00:00:56.646 --> 00:00:58.393
supplies the installed model name.

00:00:58.543 --> 00:01:04.206
Compilation verifies Java API compatibility; live model quality requires a separate evaluation

00:01:04.206 --> 00:01:06.566
on the model actually selected.

00:01:06.716 --> 00:01:10.652
The framework sample does not inherit the custom engine’s five-call limit.

00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:12.590
Compile the optional adapter.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.843
If you already have Ollama and a suitable tool-capable model, run it

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:17.184
locally.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.958
Compare the tool trace with the explicit engine.

00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:24.645
Add an application deadline and a tool-loop limit before broadening its capabilities.

00:01:24.795 --> 00:01:28.629
Use framework hooks documented for the pinned release, and test the limit

00:01:28.629 --> 00:01:31.185
with a model that repeatedly requests a tool.

00:01:31.335 --> 00:01:34.793
Preserve trusted account context outside model arguments.

00:01:34.943 --> 00:01:40.665
Treat compile success, successful inference, and safe behavior under adversarial inputs as

00:01:40.665 --> 00:01:42.095
three different results.

00:01:42.245 --> 00:01:42.917
Question.

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:46.192
Why retain a plain Java engine in a Spring AI course?

00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:46.919
Answer.

00:01:47.069 --> 00:01:53.296
It makes orchestration, validation, and stopping behavior explicit before framework abstractions hide

00:01:53.296 --> 00:01:54.334
those details.

00:01:54.484 --> 00:01:55.155
Question.

00:01:55.305 --> 00:01:57.714
Does a tool annotation authorize the caller?

00:01:57.864 --> 00:01:58.441
Answer.

00:01:58.591 --> 00:01:59.074
No.

00:01:59.224 --> 00:02:04.063
It exposes a callable capability; authorization must be implemented separately.
